Original commit body from c4bc66ed6df3df7fe9e7c6502ce2f8c41e2748aa:

`reader, writer = await asyncio.open_connection(host, 80)` hands back a
StreamReader and a StreamWriter; `proc = await
asyncio.create_subprocess_exec(...)` a Process whose stdout is a
StreamReader. py.py typed none of them -- an AWAITED call was never typed,
and a TUPLE target never was -- so `await reader.read(100)` emitted
python:external:0-0:read and reached no catalogue row: a TCP read through
open_connection was not a net_recv crossing at all.

Types come from the library-signature catalogue, which gains two sections
read only by python, beside `signatures` and go's `package_variables`:
* tuple_returns -- a producer returning a tuple, one type per position
  (`asyncio.open_connection: asyncio.StreamReader, asyncio.StreamWriter`; a
  string so every section keeps one value shape; load_library_tuple_returns
  refuses a value with fewer than two or an empty position);
* member_types -- a member attribute of a typed object
  (`asyncio.subprocess.Process.stdout: asyncio.StreamReader`).
plus signatures rows for create_subprocess_exec / _shell -> Process.

py.py: `x = await f(...)` types x from f's signatures row through the
constructor resolver (its binding check included); `a, b = await f(...)`
types each position from f's tuple_returns row (a target of another arity,
or with a starred element, is not typed by position). Binding a typed
object also binds its member types (`proc.stdout`, `proc.stdin`), on the
dotted keys _prune_shadowed and the field purge already manage.

The MEDIUM: StreamReader is one class fed by a TCP socket, an AF_UNIX socket
or a child's pipe, and only the factory knows which (WI-foloh). A small
table (_STREAM_FACTORY_MEDIA: open_connection -> net_stream;
open_unix_connection and create_subprocess_* -> pipe, as go.py stamps a
Unix socket pipe) records it at the binding, under a `\x00io_target_kind`
segment of the receiver's own dotted key (so it is pruned and purged with
the receiver, and no attribute can read it); every call through that
receiver -- `reader.read()` and the member form `proc.stdout.read()` --
carries meta io_target_kind. A rebinding of the name drops the medium.
Nothing selects on it yet: StreamReader.read* is rowed net_recv only, and
io-boundaries now classifies all three reads (TCP, Unix, child pipe)
net_recv where before none was a crossing; WI-foloh's ipc_recv twin is
what the pipe stamp will select.

Measured, analyze_python edge set, branch vs origin/dev py.py and
signature rows: mitmproxy 41, uvloop 189, meson 3, transformers 1 calls
move from the python:external placeholder to asyncio.StreamReader /
StreamWriter / asyncio.subprocess.Process (read / readuntil / readexactly /
write / drain / close / communicate / wait / kill / send_signal ...), every
one read against its line; qemu's python 0 (its `connect =
asyncio.open_connection(...)` is awaited later into `self._reader,
self._writer`, attribute targets this does not type -- a residual).

Not addressed: a name's TYPE outlives an untyped rebinding (`reader =
other`), as every py.py external type does -- the typed-assignment arms
purge only when the new value is typed; the medium is dropped regardless.

